data.anchorframe-point-plus-orientation#Ancho:
An AnchorFrame describes one local attachment coordinate frame. origin gives the local point; z gives the attachment normal and x fixes the rotation around that normal. Use it when a single point or rectangular Anchor is not enough to describe how the occurrence must be oriented.
from scpf import Anchor, AnchorFrame mount = AnchorFrame( origin=(0, 0, -12), z=(0, 1, 0), x=(0, 0, -1), ) bracket = CornerBracket().attach( side_board, parent=Anchor.FRONT_TOP_LEFT, child=mount, ) There are two ownership patterns. If the datum is an intrinsic part of a reusable component, expose it with anchors() and refer to it by name. If the frame exists only because of a particular assembly or mounting choice, construct the AnchorFrame in that assembly and pass it directly to attach(). This keeps application knowledge out of the generic component definition.
@component class Sensor: def anchors(self): return { "connector": AnchorFrame( origin=(12, 0, 4), z=(1, 0, 0), x=(0, 0, 1), ) } def build(self): return Box(24, 12, 8) sensor = Sensor().attach(panel, child="connector") For an unrotated occurrence, Forge lowers an AnchorFrame directly into the native joint.create reference as point, z and x; it does not create a persistent visible joint.origin. Named frames on explicitly rotated occurrences currently use the native origin fallback so SolidCraft owns the transform. A direct AnchorFrame on an explicitly rotated occurrence is not supported yet.
